00:12:13.990 --> 00:12:16.330
And lady, you chatted with Gwyneth Paltrow.
还有，你和格温妮丝·帕特洛聊过了。

334
00:12:16.430 --> 00:12:18.170
I look over who's so tall.
我看向那边，她太高了。

335
00:12:18.330 --> 00:12:19.030
She's very tall.
她非常高。

336
00:12:19.170 --> 00:12:20.190
Well, next to me too.
嗯，站在我旁边也是。

337
00:12:20.330 --> 00:12:20.990
Big difference.
差距很大。

338
00:12:21.510 --> 00:12:21.950
Hey.
嘿。

339
00:12:22.310 --> 00:12:24.430
Well, I mean, it was a striking difference.
我是说，那差距真的很明显。

340
00:12:24.590 --> 00:12:25.950
She's really so tall.
她真的太高了。

341
00:12:26.030 --> 00:12:28.010
And I had on three-inch heels, so.
而且我当时穿着三英寸的高跟鞋，所以。

342
00:12:28.190 --> 00:12:29.670
I'm right in the middle of you guys.
我正好在你们中间。

343
00:12:30.050 --> 00:12:31.730
Well, here's the thing.
是这样的。

344
00:12:32.330 --> 00:12:35.110
When we walked out on stage, they had an order.
当我们走上舞台时，他们安排了顺序。

345
00:12:35.270 --> 00:12:38.930
I walked out first and then Jenna and then Ellie and then Mindy, right?
我先走出来，然后是珍娜，接着是艾莉，最后是敏迪，对吧？

346
00:12:39.010 --> 00:12:39.950
We walked out in this order.
我们就是按这个顺序走出来的。

347
00:12:40.750 --> 00:12:42.710
So I'm the first one walking out there by myself.
所以我第一个独自走出来。

348
00:12:42.810 --> 00:12:49.390
And I was like, ah, the person I lock eyes with, I'm standing right in front of, is Gwyneth
当时我就想，啊，我目光锁定的那个人，我正站在她面前，是格温妮丝

349
00:12:49.390 --> 00:12:49.810
Paltrow.
帕特洛。

350
00:12:50.270 --> 00:12:54.950
And I look right at her and I make this kind of smile like, hey, and she smiled right back.
我直视着她，露出了那种微笑，像是在说“嘿”，她也回以微笑。

351
00:12:55.130 --> 00:12:58.530
And then I sort of left my body because then I looked at the whole room and I was like, oh
然后我感觉灵魂出窍了，因为我环顾整个房间，心想，天哪，

352
00:12:58.530 --> 00:12:59.470
my gosh, what are we doing?
我们在干什么？

353
00:12:59.710 --> 00:13:01.210
And I got a little nervous for a second.
那一瞬间我有点紧张。

354
00:13:01.210 --> 00:13:05.970
But after the word show was over, we were walking like towards the exit together.
但颁奖典礼结束后，我们一起往出口走去。

355
00:13:06.170 --> 00:13:09.790
And I turned to her and I said, Gwyneth, I'm Angela Kinsey.
我转过身对她说：格温妮丝，我是安吉拉·金赛。

356
00:13:09.890 --> 00:13:10.570
And she was like, hi.
她回了句：你好。

357
00:13:10.710 --> 00:13:12.130
And I said, I just wanted to say thank you.
我说：我只是想跟你说声谢谢。

358
00:13:12.290 --> 00:13:14.350
I locked eyes on you right as I walked on stage.
我走上台时正好和你对视了。

359
00:13:14.350 --> 00:13:18.210
And I was a little nervous and I kind of gave you this dorky smile and you smiled right
我当时有点紧张，还冲你露出了傻乎乎的笑容，而你也回敬了

360
00:13:18.210 --> 00:13:18.610
back.
我一个微笑。

361
00:13:18.770 --> 00:13:20.630
And it just kind of settled my nerves.
这让我平静了不少。

362
00:13:20.710 --> 00:13:23.050
And she was like, oh my goodness, you guys were fantastic.
她说：天哪，你们表现得太棒了。

363
00:13:23.330 --> 00:13:24.570
And she was really nice.
她人真的很好。

364
00:13:24.770 --> 00:13:29.550
Well, you know who I locked eyes with as I walked out on stage right in front of me?
那你知不知道我走上台时，正前方和谁对视了吗？

365
00:13:34.010 --> 00:13:41.310
And I was like, oh my God, you're a fan of his work.
我当时心想，天哪，你竟然是他的粉丝。

366
00:13:41.490 --> 00:13:45.350
But then later, like you, crazy.
但后来，就像你一样，太疯狂了。

367
00:13:45.590 --> 00:13:47.210
This is a crazy moment for me.
这对我来说真是个疯狂的时刻。

368
00:13:47.210 --> 00:13:48.890
We ended up sharing a chair.
我们最后共用了一把椅子。

369
00:13:49.230 --> 00:13:53.630
You did because we, you know how like in the commercial breaks, people get up and chat.
确实，因为你知道，在广告插播的时候，大家都会起身聊天。

370
00:13:53.810 --> 00:13:53.970
Yeah.
是啊。

371
00:13:54.150 --> 00:13:55.670
So we got up and we were chatting.
所以我们起身聊天。

372
00:13:55.870 --> 00:13:59.370
And then when the show starts back, you quickly have to sit down wherever you are.
然后节目重新开始时，你必须赶紧就近坐下。

373
00:13:59.490 --> 00:14:00.490
It's like musical chairs.
就像玩抢椅子游戏一样。

374
00:14:00.570 --> 00:14:01.070
It is.
没错。

375
00:14:01.330 --> 00:14:03.510
And I sat in Sarah Polson's chair.
我坐在了莎拉·保罗森的椅子上。

376
00:14:03.690 --> 00:14:04.310
Sarah, sorry.
莎拉，抱歉。

377
00:14:04.470 --> 00:14:05.370
I took your chair.
我占了你的椅子。

378
00:14:05.610 --> 00:14:08.010
And Sarah crouched on the ground next to me.
于是莎拉蹲在我旁边的地上。

379
00:14:08.190 --> 00:14:09.390
I was like, Sarah, I'm so sorry.
我说：莎拉，真是不好意思。

380
00:14:09.390 --> 00:14:13.750
And you quickly sat down and you and Connor sat.
然后你赶紧坐下，你和康纳坐在一起。

381
00:14:13.770 --> 00:14:14.950
We sat on the same chair.
我们坐在了同一把椅子上。

382
00:14:16.010 --> 00:14:16.810
Same chair.
同一把椅子。

383
00:14:17.490 --> 00:14:20.670
Oh, but you guys, it was just, it was amazing.
噢，但伙计们，那感觉真是太棒了。

384
00:14:20.970 --> 00:14:24.810
I'm sorry if we're going on and on, but we haven't been to one of these in a real long time.
抱歉我们一直在唠叨，但我们真的好久没参加这种活动了。

385
00:14:25.050 --> 00:14:26.250
And it was just so fun.
真的太有趣了。

386
00:14:26.370 --> 00:14:29.310
I finally got to meet Quinta Brunson, which was another highlight.
我终于见到了昆塔·布伦森，这是另一个亮点。

387
00:14:29.530 --> 00:14:32.970
And we got to hang out with Paul Mescal and Jesse Buckley.
我们还和保罗·麦斯卡以及杰西·巴克利待在一起。

388
00:14:33.190 --> 00:14:34.070
Oh my God.
天哪。

389
00:14:34.710 --> 00:14:35.150
Backstage.
在后台。

390
00:14:35.450 --> 00:14:37.290
Jesse Buckley offered to take our picture.
杰西·巴克利主动提出帮我们拍照。

391
00:14:37.430 --> 00:14:38.470
I was like, what is happening?
我心想，这到底是怎么回事？

392
00:14:39.010 --> 00:14:42.290
Yes, we were like, can we get a picture with you?
是的，我们当时说：能和你合张影吗？

393
00:14:44.110 --> 00:14:48.150
And at the party afterwards, we saw Rose Byrne, who I love.
在之后的派对上，我们见到了我很喜欢的萝丝·拜恩。

394
00:14:48.330 --> 00:14:50.510
And Catherine Hahn, who I've been friends with for so long.
还有我认识很久的朋友凯瑟琳·哈恩。

395
00:14:50.670 --> 00:14:53.250
And Rhea Sehorne was at our table with her son.
蕾亚·塞洪也带着她儿子坐在我们那桌。

396
00:14:53.370 --> 00:14:53.950
With her son.
带着她儿子。

397
00:14:54.330 --> 00:14:54.690
Mm-hmm.
嗯。

398
00:14:54.850 --> 00:14:55.790
She was wonderful.
她人真是太好了。

399
00:14:56.050 --> 00:14:56.830
And Sterling K.
还有斯特林·K·

400
00:14:56.970 --> 00:14:57.230
Brown.
布朗。

401
00:14:57.310 --> 00:14:58.570
Who called me Ange.
是谁叫我Ange的。

402
00:14:58.690 --> 00:14:59.130
I know.
我知道。

403
00:14:59.270 --> 00:15:01.750
And I was like, Sterling, my family calls me Ange.
当时我就说，Sterling，我家人都叫我Ange。

404
00:15:01.810 --> 00:15:03.070
How do you know people call me Ange?
你怎么知道别人叫我Ange的？

405
00:15:03.110 --> 00:15:05.510
And he said, well, my sister's named Angela and I call her Ange.
他说，哦，我妹妹叫Angela，我就叫她Ange。

406
00:15:05.550 --> 00:15:06.170
Well, there you go.
好吧，原来是这样。

407
00:15:06.310 --> 00:15:08.410
And I was like, Sterling, you can call me Ange forever.
当时我就想，Sterling，你可以一直叫我Ange。

408
00:15:08.470 --> 00:15:10.130
We've dropped so many names.
我们提到了好多人的名字。

409
00:15:10.410 --> 00:15:10.890
We have.
确实。

410
00:15:11.330 --> 00:15:11.790
Sorry.
抱歉。

411
00:15:11.970 --> 00:15:13.690
But all of these people were wonderful.
但这些人个个都很棒。

412
00:15:13.690 --> 00:15:16.070
So if you want to know what they're like, the answer is wonderful.
所以如果你想知道他们是什么样的人，答案就是：他们都很棒。

413
00:15:16.270 --> 00:15:24.750
And maybe most wonderful of all, not that it's a contest, sweet darling Oliver Hudson with the crazy tattoo.
或许最棒的——虽然这不是在比赛——是那个有着疯狂纹身的甜心Oliver Hudson。

414
00:15:24.750 --> 00:15:26.090
We ran into him.
我们碰巧遇到了他。

415
00:15:26.650 --> 00:15:28.070
He's so fun.
他太有趣了。

416
00:15:28.370 --> 00:15:29.550
And here's the thing about Oliver.
关于Oliver，有一点是这样的。

417
00:15:29.710 --> 00:15:30.970
You know him way better than I do.
你比我更了解他。

418
00:15:31.130 --> 00:15:32.410
Obviously, y'all did show together.
显然，你们一起拍过戏。

419
00:15:32.530 --> 00:15:36.410
But when you meet him, he's one of those people where you feel like you've known him your whole life.
但当你见到他时，你会觉得仿佛认识他一辈子了。

420
00:15:36.590 --> 00:15:36.810
Yeah.
没错。

421
00:15:36.890 --> 00:15:37.410
He's so.
他就是那么……

422
00:15:37.410 --> 00:15:37.930
No affect.
不做作。

423
00:15:37.930 --> 00:15:40.530
No, he's just so easy to be around.
没错，和他相处太轻松了。

424
00:15:40.790 --> 00:15:43.210
And he told me, this is what's crazy.
他告诉我一件事，这太疯狂了。

425
00:15:43.430 --> 00:15:44.190
Guess what?
你猜怎么着？

426
00:15:44.350 --> 00:15:47.490
I said that this night was full of fabulous women.
我说过今晚到处都是了不起的女性。

427
00:15:47.830 --> 00:15:48.730
Guess what?
你猜怎么着？

428
00:15:48.910 --> 00:15:52.770
Books made their way in because Oliver Hudson is a voracious reader.
书也成了话题，因为Oliver Hudson是个如饥似渴的读者。

429
00:15:53.290 --> 00:15:53.690
Oh, yeah.
哦，没错。

430
00:15:53.850 --> 00:16:01.370
We actually got, I won't say reprimanded is the right word, but there was a security man that said, you need to leave now.
我们其实被——我不确定“训斥”这个词是否准确，但有个保安说，你们现在得走了。

431
00:16:01.570 --> 00:16:06.190
Because everyone had to exit into the party that you spill into this party next door.
因为每个人都得退场到隔壁的派对里去。

432
00:16:06.630 --> 00:16:09.350
And you two were talking about the books you were reading.
而你们俩当时还在聊正在读的书。

433
00:16:09.630 --> 00:16:11.550
And you were so invested in this conversation.
你们聊得太投入了。

434
00:16:11.550 --> 00:16:13.950
I was like, y'all need to walk and talk.
我当时就说，你们得边走边聊啊。

435
00:16:14.170 --> 00:16:14.310
Yeah.
没错。

436
00:16:14.810 --> 00:16:18.370
And then the next day, just texting about books.
然后第二天，我们还在发短信聊书。

437
00:16:18.570 --> 00:16:21.950
So Oliver is going to be my book friend this year, I think.
所以我觉得，Oliver今年会成为我的书友。

438
00:16:21.950 --> 00:16:23.770
He also said he's going to come on the podcaSt.
他还说他会来上我们的播客。

439
00:16:24.050 --> 00:16:26.170
We think we need to play two truths and a lie with him.
我觉得我们得和他玩玩“两真一假”。

440
00:16:26.310 --> 00:16:27.150
I think we do, too.
我也这么觉得。

441
00:16:27.690 --> 00:16:28.030
All right.
好吧。

442
00:16:28.430 --> 00:16:32.630
Congratulations on your birthday theme of fabulous women and books.
祝贺你的生日主题是“了不起的女性与书”。

443
00:16:32.870 --> 00:16:33.310
Yes.
是的。

444
00:16:33.530 --> 00:16:35.770
We have some fun surprises planned for you.
我们为你准备了一些有趣的惊喜。

445
00:16:35.890 --> 00:16:38.830
I think we should take a quick break, have some birthday cake.
我想我们该休息一下，吃点生日蛋糕了。

446
00:16:38.890 --> 00:16:43.170
And when we come back, we're going to do the call sheet questions in honor of your birthday.
等回来之后，我们要为了庆祝你的生日，来回答“通告单”上的问题。

447
00:16:43.690 --> 00:16:43.970
Great.
太棒了。

448
00:16:50.840 --> 00:16:54.060
Attention all new and existing members of the WDNC.
请WDNC的所有新老成员注意。

449
00:16:54.320 --> 00:16:57.900
I'm Melanie Sanders, and this is the We Do Not Care Club podcaSt.
我是Melanie Sanders，这里是《我们不在乎俱乐部》播客。

450
00:16:58.060 --> 00:17:02.140
We got to midlife and realized we just simply do not care much anymore.
我们到了中年才意识到，我们真的已经不在乎那么多了。

451
00:17:02.420 --> 00:17:10.240
Now we got to help each other make sense of hot flashes, itchy ears, chin hair, trifling families, brain fog, and bat wings.
现在我们得互相帮助，去理解潮热、耳朵发痒、下巴长毛、琐碎的家庭事务、脑雾以及蝴蝶袖这些问题。

452
00:17:10.500 --> 00:17:13.120
We are not alone, and we will be heard.
我们并不孤单，我们的声音会被听见。

453
00:17:13.300 --> 00:17:15.000
So come join the WDNC.
所以，加入我们“不在乎俱乐部”（WDNC）吧。

454
00:17:15.300 --> 00:17:20.400
Follow and listen to the We Do Not Care Club podcast with Melanie Sanders wherever you get your podcaSt.
在各大播客平台关注并收听梅兰妮·桑德斯主持的《不在乎俱乐部》播客。

455
00:17:24.390 --> 00:17:24.870
All right.
好的。

456
00:17:24.870 --> 00:17:25.870
We are back.
我们回来了。

457
00:17:25.870 --> 00:17:30.610
And before we kick off the call sheet questions, I have two fun things we're going to do in honor of your birthday.
在开始讨论提纲上的问题之前，为了庆祝你的生日，我们要进行两项有趣的活动。

458
00:17:30.890 --> 00:17:31.130
Okay.
好的。

459
00:17:31.570 --> 00:17:39.430
The first thing is I'm going to share with you some trivia from March of 1974, the year you were born, Ms. Fisher.
第一项是，费舍尔女士，我要和你分享一些1974年3月，也就是你出生那年的冷知识。

460
00:17:41.430 --> 00:17:49.430
The number one grossing movie, the month of March in 1974, was The Great Gatsby, starring Robert Redford and Mia Farrow.
1974年3月票房最高的电影是罗伯特·雷德福和米亚·法罗主演的《了不起的盖茨比》。

461
00:17:49.730 --> 00:17:50.690
Oh, wow.
噢，哇。

462
00:17:51.090 --> 00:17:52.410
You know I've never seen it.
你知道我从来没看过那部电影。

463
00:17:52.910 --> 00:17:53.330
Really?
真的吗？

464
00:17:53.330 --> 00:17:55.370
Yeah, I saw the Leo DiCaprio one.
是啊，我看过莱昂纳多·迪卡普里奥演的那版。

465
00:17:55.650 --> 00:17:59.330
You should see the original since it was the big hit, The Month You Were Born.
你应该看看原版，毕竟那是你出生那月最火的电影。

466
00:17:59.510 --> 00:17:59.990
Done.
没问题。

467
00:18:00.170 --> 00:18:01.330
I'm going to do it this weekend.
我这个周末就看。

468
00:18:01.690 --> 00:18:09.770
The top television shows, The Month You Were Born in 1974, were All in the Family and Sanford and Son.
你出生那月，也就是1974年3月，最热门的电视节目是《全家福》和《桑福德和儿子》。

469
00:18:10.290 --> 00:18:10.770
Wow.
哇。

470
00:18:11.030 --> 00:18:11.450
Mm-hmm.
嗯哼。

471
00:18:11.690 --> 00:18:13.630
Those are two really cool shows.
那两部剧真的很棒。

472
00:18:13.970 --> 00:18:20.370
The number one song, The Month You Were Born, was Seasons in the Sun by Terry Jacks.
你出生那月，排名第一的歌曲是特里·杰克斯的《阳光季节》。

473
00:18:20.370 --> 00:18:29.310
Well, the music was moody, the film was kind of romantic and over the top, and the television was very cutting edge.
嗯，那时的音乐忧郁，电影浪漫且夸张，电视节目则非常前卫。

474
00:18:29.490 --> 00:18:29.890
Yes.
没错。

475
00:18:30.310 --> 00:18:30.590
Hmm.
嗯。

476
00:18:31.010 --> 00:18:31.830
I like it.
我喜欢。

477
00:18:32.210 --> 00:18:35.090
I like the artistry of my birth year.
我喜欢我出生年份的艺术气息。

478
00:18:35.090 --> 00:18:37.450
You were meant to be an artist, is what it tells me.
这说明你注定要成为一名艺术家。

479
00:18:37.910 --> 00:18:40.790
All right, and now are you ready for a little birthday surprise?
好了，现在准备好迎接一个小小的生日惊喜了吗？

480
00:18:40.790 --> 00:18:42.150
We've had birthday trivia.
刚才我们聊了生日冷知识。

481
00:18:42.370 --> 00:18:43.670
Now it's birthday surprise.
现在是生日惊喜环节。

482
00:18:43.950 --> 00:18:44.310
Okay.
好的。

483
00:18:44.750 --> 00:18:48.670
I reached out to Matt and Sam, and I said, Jenna loves books.
我联系了马特和萨姆，我说，珍娜很喜欢书。

484
00:18:48.910 --> 00:18:50.790
This is part of her birthday year theme.
这是她生日年主题的一部分。

485
00:18:51.070 --> 00:18:51.370
Mm-hmm.
嗯哼。

486
00:18:52.350 --> 00:18:58.210
I want us each to read a little passage from a book that we love, and that means something to us.
我想让我们每个人都读一段自己喜爱且意义非凡的书中的文字。

487
00:18:58.310 --> 00:18:59.290
I love this.
我太喜欢这个主意了。

488
00:18:59.590 --> 00:19:03.250
And then, lady, you are going to be getting the books we talked about.
然后，女士，你将会收到我们提到的这些书。

489
00:19:03.810 --> 00:19:04.990
This is so exciting.
这太让人兴奋了。

490
00:19:05.330 --> 00:19:05.770
Yes.
是的。

491
00:19:05.990 --> 00:19:07.990
All right, Sam, why don't you go first?
好了，萨姆，你先来好吗？

492
00:19:08.790 --> 00:19:09.230
Sure.
没问题。

493
00:19:09.830 --> 00:19:11.830
Okay, so this is a poem, actually.
好的，其实这是一首诗。

494
00:19:11.830 --> 00:19:15.690
It's only six paragraphs long, but I've heard the end of it my entire life,
它只有六段，我一生中一直听过它的结尾，

495
00:19:15.730 --> 00:19:17.870
but I just recently heard the entire thing.
但直到最近我才听过完整版。

496
00:19:17.870 --> 00:19:18.950
It's by Dylan Thomas.
作者是迪伦·托马斯。

497
00:19:19.090 --> 00:19:21.790
It's called Do Not Go Gentle Into That Good Night.
诗名是《不要温和地走进那个良夜》。

498
00:19:22.290 --> 00:19:22.930
It says,
它是这样写的：

499
00:19:23.670 --> 00:19:25.150
Do not go gentle into that good night.
不要温和地走进那个良夜，

500
00:19:25.430 --> 00:19:28.110
Old age should burn and rave at close of day.
老年应当在日暮时燃烧咆哮。

501
00:19:28.450 --> 00:19:30.290
Rage, rage against the dying of the light.
愤怒吧，愤怒地抗拒那垂死的微光。

502
00:19:30.550 --> 00:19:32.830
Though wise men at the end know dark is right,
尽管智者在临终时明白黑暗是必然，

503
00:19:32.870 --> 00:19:34.750
because their words had forked no lightning,
因为他们的言语未能如闪电般劈开黑暗，

504
00:19:35.030 --> 00:19:37.010
they do not go gentle into that good night.
他们绝不温顺地走进那良夜。

505
00:19:37.370 --> 00:19:39.350
Good men, the last wave by,
善良的人，当最后一波浪潮涌过，

506
00:19:39.690 --> 00:19:42.830
crying how bright their feral deeds have danced in a green bay.
呼喊着他们狂野的行径曾在绿湾中跳跃得多么灿烂。

507
00:19:43.090 --> 00:19:44.770
Rage against the dying of the light.
愤怒地抗拒那垂死的微光。

508
00:19:45.070 --> 00:19:47.690
Wild men who caught and sang in the sun in flight,
狂野的人，捕捉并歌唱着飞逝的太阳，

509
00:19:47.870 --> 00:19:49.950
we learned too late, they grieved on its way.
我们太晚才明白，他们曾为它的离去而悲伤。

510
00:19:50.130 --> 00:19:51.990
Do not go gentle into that good night.
不要温顺地走进那良夜。

511
00:19:52.350 --> 00:19:55.270
Grave men near death, who see with blinding light,
严肃的人，临近死亡，以刺眼的光芒洞察，

512
00:19:55.590 --> 00:19:57.810
blind eyes could blaze like meteors and gay.
失明的双眼也能像流星般燃烧、欢愉。

513
00:19:58.070 --> 00:19:59.670
Rage against the dying of the light.
愤怒地抗拒那垂死的微光。

514
00:19:59.850 --> 00:20:02.050
And you, my father, there in the sad height,
而你，我的父亲，在那悲伤的高处，

515
00:20:02.350 --> 00:20:05.450
curse, bless me now with your fierce tears, I pray.
诅咒我，祝福我，用你那炽热的泪水，我祈求。

516
00:20:05.730 --> 00:20:07.510
Do not go gentle into that good night.
不要温顺地走进那良夜。

517
00:20:07.750 --> 00:20:09.130
Rage against the dying of the light.
愤怒地抗拒那垂死的微光。

518
00:20:09.130 --> 00:20:14.570
So that was written by Dylan Thomas in, I want to say, 1940.
所以那是迪伦·托马斯写的，我想大概是在1940年。

519
00:20:15.190 --> 00:20:16.910
But he reminded me of you.
但他让我想起了你。

520
00:20:17.130 --> 00:20:19.050
I am naturally very complacent.
我天生就很随遇而安。

521
00:20:19.190 --> 00:20:20.190
It's a Wisconsin upbringing.
这是威斯康星州养成的性格。

522
00:20:20.330 --> 00:20:21.070
I'm very docile.
我非常温顺。

523
00:20:21.250 --> 00:20:24.190
I very easily will fall in line with things.
我很容易就会顺从于事物。

524
00:20:24.370 --> 00:20:27.930
But you strike me as somebody who knows exactly what they want out of life,
但你给我的感觉是，你非常清楚自己想要什么样的人生，

525
00:20:28.170 --> 00:20:32.630
is very particular, and isn't afraid to change the things in their life to get what they want.
非常有主见，而且不害怕为了得到自己想要的而去改变生活中的事物。

526
00:20:32.770 --> 00:20:34.550
I've always really admired that about you.
我一直非常钦佩你这一点。

527
00:20:34.550 --> 00:20:39.370
I think in this 52 years you've been alive, I think you were successfully raging against the
我想在你活着的这52年里，我觉得你一直在成功地抗拒那

528
00:20:39.370 --> 00:20:39.850
dying light.
垂死的微光。

529
00:20:41.470 --> 00:20:44.330
Sam, first of all, I love that you picked poetry,
山姆，首先，我很高兴你选了诗歌，

530
00:20:44.910 --> 00:20:49.250
because it is a sect of literature that I'm less familiar with.
因为这是我不太熟悉的文学流派。

531
00:20:49.310 --> 00:20:49.710
Yeah, me too.
是啊，我也是。

532
00:20:49.850 --> 00:20:51.110
And I would love to know more.
我很想了解更多。

533
00:20:51.210 --> 00:20:53.970
So I'm excited that poetry is coming my way.
所以我很兴奋能接触到诗歌。

534
00:20:54.150 --> 00:20:56.390
You're getting the book, The Poems of Dylan Thomas.
你要收到的书是《迪伦·托马斯诗集》。

535
00:20:56.710 --> 00:20:58.710
Oh, and I love that poem.
哦，我喜欢那首诗。

536
00:20:59.070 --> 00:21:02.590
And everything you just said, that just means so much to me.
你刚才说的一切，对我来说意义重大。

537
00:21:02.730 --> 00:21:02.970
Well, great.
嗯，太好了。

538
00:21:03.070 --> 00:21:03.490
Happy birthday.
生日快乐。

539
00:21:03.490 --> 00:21:03.950
I love you.
我爱你。

540
00:21:03.950 --> 00:21:04.590
Thank you.
谢谢你。

541
00:21:04.690 --> 00:21:05.370
I love you.
我也爱你。

542
00:21:05.910 --> 00:21:07.810
That was so beautiful, Sam.
那真是太美了，山姆。

543
00:21:08.030 --> 00:21:08.570
Damn you.
该死。

544
00:21:08.970 --> 00:21:10.730
But Matt, you have a book.
但是马特，你有一本书。

545
00:21:11.030 --> 00:21:13.170
Now, this is a book Jenna already has.
这本书珍娜已经有了。

546
00:21:13.350 --> 00:21:15.210
So Jenna, you're going to be getting two books.
所以珍娜，你会收到两本书。

547
00:21:15.310 --> 00:21:15.550
Okay.
好的。

548
00:21:15.730 --> 00:21:17.570
Because this one we believe you have.
因为我们相信你已经有这一本了。

549
00:21:17.790 --> 00:21:19.190
But Matt, I'll let you take it from there.
但是马特，接下来的交给你了。

550
00:21:19.790 --> 00:21:20.510
Well, yeah.
嗯，好的。

551
00:21:20.670 --> 00:21:25.510
When I first came on the show, we were reading the same book, A Marriage at Sea.
我刚上这个节目时，我们当时在读同一本书，《海上的婚姻》。

552
00:21:25.730 --> 00:21:30.570
And I really thought that was so cool, You, Me, and Barack Obama, because it was on his
我觉得这太酷了，你、我和巴拉克·奥巴马，因为这本书在他当年的

553
00:21:30.570 --> 00:21:31.450
book list for the year.
书单上。

554
00:21:31.470 --> 00:21:31.670
Oh.
噢。

555
00:21:31.910 --> 00:21:33.010
But I just thought that was awesome.
但我就是觉得这太棒了。

556
00:21:33.950 --> 00:21:35.550
We have a shared love of this book.
我们对这本书有着共同的喜爱。

557
00:21:35.670 --> 00:21:36.610
We're reading it at the same time.
我们正在同时阅读它。

558
00:21:36.770 --> 00:21:38.010
So I picked a quote from that.
所以我从中选了一段引语。

559
00:21:38.170 --> 00:21:43.550
And this is from A Marriage at Sea by Sophie Elmhurst, which was a great book about a couple
这段话出自苏菲·埃尔姆赫斯特的《海上的婚姻》，那是一本关于一对夫妇

560
00:21:43.550 --> 00:21:44.890
surviving at sea together.
在海上共同求生的好书。

561
00:21:44.890 --> 00:21:54.990
And the quote is, I believe in all human beings, there is a desire to love and be loved, to experience the full fierceness of human emotion,
引语是这样的：我相信在所有人类心中，都有一种去爱与被爱的渴望，去体验人类情感的全部炽热，

562
00:21:54.990 --> 00:21:58.470
and to make it a measure of the success of one's life.
并将其作为衡量一个人一生是否成功的标准。

563
00:21:59.010 --> 00:22:02.870
And I really love that, to quote, rent, to measure your life and love.
我真的很喜欢这一点，引用、租借，去衡量你生命中的爱。

564
00:22:03.830 --> 00:22:07.370
So yeah, I hope it's a wonderful year for you with lots of love.
所以，是的，希望这对你来说是充满爱的一年。

565
00:22:07.370 --> 00:22:08.590
Thank you, Matt.
谢谢你，马特。

566
00:22:08.750 --> 00:22:11.950
And I love that you threw a little theater into there as well.
而且我很喜欢你还在里面加了一点戏剧感。

567
00:22:12.210 --> 00:22:13.350
That's, I love it.
那真的，我很喜欢。

568
00:22:13.570 --> 00:22:15.370
Yeah, that, that's a beautiful passage.
是的，那，那是一段优美的文字。

569
00:22:15.530 --> 00:22:17.110
And that book is really incredible.
而且那本书真的很不可思议。

570
00:22:17.350 --> 00:22:21.390
And now I know if I ever meet Barack Obama, I can bring up this book.
现在我知道了，如果我哪天见到巴拉克·奥巴马，我可以提起这本书。

571
00:22:21.530 --> 00:22:22.150
You can talk books.
你可以聊聊书。

572
00:22:22.150 --> 00:22:22.410
Yeah.
是的。

573
00:22:24.330 --> 00:22:25.170
Thank you.
谢谢。

574
00:22:25.510 --> 00:22:26.630
Okay, Angela, your turn.
好了，安吉拉，轮到你了。

575
00:22:26.930 --> 00:22:27.990
Okay, my turn.
好的，轮到我了。

576
00:22:28.350 --> 00:22:32.710
So I brought a book in because, you know, I love show and tell.
我带了一本书来，因为你知道，我喜欢“展示与讲述”环节。

577
00:22:33.250 --> 00:22:37.510
But this book is really special to me because it was one of my dad's favorite books.
但这本书对我来说真的很特别，因为这是我爸爸最喜欢的书之一。

578
00:22:38.070 --> 00:22:41.830
And when he passed away, my mom gave me a bunch of his favorite books.
他去世后，我妈妈给了我一堆他最喜欢的书。

579
00:22:41.950 --> 00:22:43.330
My dad was always reading a book.
我爸爸总是书不离手。

580
00:22:43.510 --> 00:22:49.510
He had a lot of long hours that he traveled back and forth to the rig, and he would read when he traveled.
他经常要花很长时间往返于钻井平台，他在旅途中就会看书。

581
00:22:49.750 --> 00:22:51.610
So this is one of his favorite books.
所以这是他最喜欢的书之一。

582
00:22:52.150 --> 00:22:53.930
It's a connection to my hometown as well.
它也与我的家乡有着某种联系。

583
00:22:54.090 --> 00:23:00.170
This is my dad's copy of Lonesome Dove by Larry McMurtry, and it won the Pulitzer.
这是我爸爸那本拉里·麦克穆特里的《孤鸽镇》，它曾获得普利策奖。

584
00:23:00.470 --> 00:23:03.470
So this was really amazing for me to go back.
所以对我来说，重读这本书真的很奇妙。

585
00:23:03.730 --> 00:23:04.910
Written in here, it says,
上面写着：

586
00:23:05.050 --> 00:23:10.190
Given to Billy Kinsey for his 50th birthday in Archer City, Texas on July 26, 1985.
赠予比利·金西，作为他50岁生日礼物，1985年7月26日于德克萨斯州阿彻城。

587
00:23:11.110 --> 00:23:13.570
And I don't think you have this book.
我想你还没读过这本书吧。

588
00:23:13.730 --> 00:23:14.550
I don't.
我没读过。

589
00:23:14.650 --> 00:23:14.890
Okay.
好吧。

590
00:23:15.310 --> 00:23:21.890
So it's this epic Western novel written by Larry McMurtry, who's from Archer City, from my hometown.
这是一部史诗般的西部小说，作者拉里·麦克穆特里就来自我的家乡阿彻城。

591
00:23:22.150 --> 00:23:25.850
And we all grew up knowing his family.
我们从小就认识他的家人。

592
00:23:26.130 --> 00:23:28.510
My mom is very good friends with his sister.
我妈妈和他妹妹是非常好的朋友。

593
00:23:28.770 --> 00:23:30.670
I went to school with his nephew.
我和他的侄子是同学。

594
00:23:30.990 --> 00:23:33.070
My dad went to school with Larry for a while.
我爸爸曾和拉里做过一段时间同学。

595
00:23:33.190 --> 00:23:35.590
So this is very rooted in our hometown.
所以这本书深深扎根于我们的家乡。

596
00:23:36.210 --> 00:23:43.810
But more than that, the book is just a really beautiful story about love and loss and friendship and the end of the Old WeSt.
但不仅如此，这本书讲述了一个关于爱、失去、友谊以及旧西部终结的非常动人的故事。

597
00:23:43.810 --> 00:23:46.310
It's got a huge, large cast of characters.
它有着庞大的人物阵容。

598
00:23:46.710 --> 00:23:52.270
And it really centers around Augustus McRae and Woodrow Call, who are these Texas Rangers.
故事主要围绕着奥古斯都·麦克雷和伍德罗·考尔这两位德克萨斯骑警展开。

599
00:23:52.270 --> 00:23:56.230
And they're on this very dangerous cattle drive from Texas to Montana.
他们正在进行一场从德克萨斯到蒙大拿的极其危险的赶牛之旅。

600
00:23:56.230 --> 00:24:03.870
But there's so much love and life that happens to them and the people around them along the way.
但在这一路上，他们以及周围的人经历了如此多的爱与生活。

601
00:24:04.150 --> 00:24:07.050
And there are two things that I like.
我有两样喜欢的东西。

602
00:24:07.170 --> 00:24:09.970
And I find that I like the things Augustus says.
而且我发现我很喜欢奥古斯塔斯说的话。

603
00:24:10.410 --> 00:24:12.470
He's such a bigger-than-life personality.
他是一个非常有感染力的人物。

604
00:24:12.470 --> 00:24:15.610
But one of the things he says in the book that I liked is,
但他在书中说的一句话我很喜欢，那就是：

605
00:24:15.870 --> 00:24:19.390
if you want one thing too much, it's likely to be a disappointment.
如果你对某件事渴求得太过强烈，它往往会让你失望。

606
00:24:19.670 --> 00:24:26.570
The healthy way is to learn to like the everyday things, like soft beds and buttermilk and feisty gentlemen.
健康的生活方式是学会享受日常琐事，比如柔软的床铺、酪乳和充满活力的绅士。

607
00:24:26.570 --> 00:24:32.130
And then the other one, a lot of people, like when you look up, like quotes that people love from this book,
还有另一句话，很多人在搜索这本书里大家最喜欢的名言时，

608
00:24:32.250 --> 00:24:34.310
this is one that a lot of people say.
都会提到这一句。

609
00:24:34.490 --> 00:24:37.770
But I loved the idea of just being comfortable with everyday things.
但我特别喜欢那种对日常琐事感到满足的理念。

610
00:24:37.950 --> 00:24:38.990
And that is you to me.
而你给我的感觉就是这样。

611
00:24:39.210 --> 00:24:43.850
You really enjoy just your cup of coffee in the morning makes you so happy.
你真的很享受早晨的那杯咖啡，它能让你感到无比快乐。

612
00:24:44.010 --> 00:24:44.590
It does.
确实如此。

613
00:24:44.770 --> 00:24:49.270
Sitting in your cozy nook and reading a book or going for a walk with Lee.
坐在舒适的角落里看书，或者和李一起去散步。

614
00:24:49.510 --> 00:24:52.530
These are the things that just fill your bucket, right?
这些就是让你感到充实的事情，对吧？

615
00:24:52.530 --> 00:24:57.510
An afternoon with your kids, just hanging out in the backyard with your dog, Maggie.
和孩子们共度午后，或者只是和你的狗玛吉在后院闲逛。

616
00:24:57.750 --> 00:25:00.630
And just those little moments are you.
这些小小的瞬间就是你的写照。

617
00:25:01.070 --> 00:25:04.610
And this one is one other quote I'll share.
还有这一句名言我也想分享一下。

618
00:25:04.890 --> 00:25:06.850
It ain't dying I'm talking about.
我说的不是死亡。

619
00:25:06.850 --> 00:25:07.430
It's living.
而是生活。

620
00:25:07.650 --> 00:25:11.010
I doubt it matters where you die, but it matters where you live.
我想在哪里死去并不重要，重要的是你如何生活。

621
00:25:11.270 --> 00:25:18.190
And I see you embrace that all the time, especially the journey you've been on the last few years.
我看到你一直都在践行这一点，尤其是你过去几年所经历的旅程。

622
00:25:18.350 --> 00:25:20.530
You know, you want to make now matter.
你知道，你想要让当下变得有意义。

623
00:25:20.530 --> 00:25:21.530
Mm-hmm.
嗯。

624
00:25:21.870 --> 00:25:22.710
Oh, wow.
噢，哇。

625
00:25:23.050 --> 00:25:23.330
Yeah.
是啊。

626
00:25:23.530 --> 00:25:25.830
So you will be getting a copy of Lonesome Dove.
所以你会收到一本《孤独的鸽子》。

627
00:25:25.950 --> 00:25:26.810
I'm very excited.
我太激动了。

628
00:25:26.810 --> 00:25:31.850
You know, I particularly love stories like on a prairie.
你知道，我特别喜欢那种发生在草原上的故事。

629
00:25:32.110 --> 00:25:34.390
I love prairie life stories.
我热爱草原生活的故事。

630
00:25:34.730 --> 00:25:34.970
I didn't know that.
我之前不知道这一点。

631
00:25:34.970 --> 00:25:39.530
Well, they have a very kind of survivalist aspect to them.
嗯，它们有一种生存主义的色彩。

632
00:25:39.590 --> 00:25:39.910
Yes.
是的。

633
00:25:40.110 --> 00:25:45.490
Because like so much of the day is just about gathering food.
因为一天中大部分时间都在忙着寻找食物。

634
00:25:46.030 --> 00:25:46.630
Survival.
生存。

635
00:25:46.630 --> 00:25:50.510
Yes, you know, and cooking the food and trading the food.
是的，你知道，还有烹饪食物和交换食物。

636
